                  <provenance type="#i"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_96">Cambridge, Cambridgeshire.
                     Convent of Austin friars</orgName><p>Copied by Adam de Stockton, lector in the
                     Austin convent at Cambridge c. 1375: 'scriptus a fratre Adam de Stocton lectore
                     in conuentu Cantebrigie a.d. mccc septuagesimo septimus' (p. 361), and in other
                     places in the MS in other years (BRUC 557).</p><p>'Liber iste pertinet fratri
                     Roberto Mendham anno domini MºCCCC nonagesimo vijº' (at end). [i.e. Robert
                     Mendham OESA, Cambridge convent in 1470–71; in 1508; BRUC 401–2.]</p><p>'Liber
                     iste pertinet fratri Thome Swyllyngton anno domini mºcccccº vº ita est per me
                     prescript'' (at end). [i.e. Thomas Swillington OP, Cambridge convent in 1408;
                     BRUC 571.]</p><p>Also 'conuentus . . . . brigie', erased.</p><p>
                     </p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_94">Cambridge,
                     Cambridgeshire, Franciscan convent</orgName> <p>This attribution was rejected
                     in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did
                     not change in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval Libraries of
                     Great Britain index card offers the following context for the
                     rejection:</p>Conjecture of provenance by James, <title>Library of Dr John
                     Dee.</title> But see Esposito in <title>EHR</title> XXXIII (1918), 359-66, and
                     Tanner's <title>Biblioteca</title>, from which it appears that Adam de Stockton
                     was lector in the Augustinian convent at Cambridge.<p/></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">John Dee</persName>
                     (1527–1609), mathematician, astrologer, and antiquary: <q>Ioannes Dee 1561
                     Bostoniae</q> (fol. 5r). Dee's cat. C, no. 7. Compare Oxford, Corpus Christi
                     College, MS 228.</provenance>
               </history>
